  },
  "holistic_F_N": 1,
  "holistic_F_H": -1,
  "section_notes": "S1, S2, S4 and S5 land inside the acceptable ranges: fast reporting and notice deadlines (24h serious / 5 days other / 5 days customer), a 55 °C test with a permanent 5% cap plus a bounded 6-month corrective-and-retest window, built-in suppression at 36 months, and a mandatory numeric recall at 8 per 100,000 with 5-day notices and no transition. The main departure from the answer key is S3, where new units only need shut-off 24 months after adoption (acceptable range is ≤12 months), even though the 6-year retrofit deadline is inside the acceptable range. Red lines are all respected: per-model results go to regulators and purchasers but not the general public, and the 6-year retrofit and 36-month suppression dates satisfy NW-2 and HC-2. S5's hard mandatory recall at 8/100k with no phase-in is the provision most adverse to Halcyon (Ember 8 at 9.9), while Northwind's Hearth 10 (6.2% at 55 °C) gets a regulator-supervised corrective pathway rather than an outright ban, so the package tilts slightly to Northwind."
 },
